<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 39 § 1: "edited in the editor as a plain inspector over the node's [DataContract] members.
    Hot reload already works; the panel is a form, not new machinery." This is that form, and it is
    markup with no code-behind for the reason doc 36 § P4 gives — `PropertyField` resolves each
    member against the ambient edit target and hands it to whatever drawer claims it, so the reset
    button, the tooltip, the mixed state and the undo all arrive without being asked for.

    ⚠ **The grouping is the argument, not decoration.** The generated inspector would draw eight
    rows in declaration order, and eight semantic knobs read as one undifferentiated column is
    exactly the "which one do I turn" problem the Standard Frame exists to solve. The three groups
    are the three questions a person actually arrives with: how the frame is lit, what it costs, and
    where it comes out.

    ⚠ **Quality is first and alone.** It is the only knob that changes sixty-two other numbers, and
    the resolved-stack panel below the form is the readout for what it did — see
    `ResolvedQualityTable`. Putting it in with the lighting switches would bury the one control whose
    effect is not local.
-->
@component StandardFrameInspector
@namespace Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors.Frame
@tag standard-frame-inspector
@using Vixen.Editor.Inspector
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls

<Expander Label="Scalability" IsExpanded="true">
    <PropertyField Path="Quality" />
</Expander>

<Expander Label="Lighting" IsExpanded="true">
    <PropertyField Path="Shadows" />
    <PropertyField Path="Gi" />
    <PropertyField Path="Reflections" />
</Expander>

<Expander Label="Image" IsExpanded="true">
    <PropertyField Path="Antialiasing" />
    <PropertyField Path="Exposure" />
    <PropertyField Path="Particles" />
</Expander>

<Expander Label="Output" IsExpanded="false">
    <PropertyField Path="Output" />
</Expander>
